+# Agent Note: Opt-in illustrated character themes
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-17-opt-in-illustrated-character-themes.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+ThemeRuntime can register semantic-token overrides, but an illustrated character theme also needs a full-frame image and a recognizable decorative element. Letting a feature package inject global selectors would create a second theme authority beside ui-theme, while putting artwork in the Web frontend's public directory would bind an independently loadable plugin to one application dist. A character theme is also a personal presentation choice and cannot alter the shipped Web default.
+
+## Decision
+
+ui-theme owns four scheme-independent application-background aliases: image, overlay, position, and size. ui-layout's AppFrame paints the overlay above the image behind its columns. Their built-in values preserve the solid application background, so features consume the same frame without a theme branch. This extends the existing [Web styling system](../process/2026-07-19-web-styling-system.md); it does not replace its CSS Modules, semantic-token, or single-global-owner rules.
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-fang-yuan-theme` is an independently loadable client plugin. The Web bundle declares its Host row disabled, and the example overlay enables that row by id. The client half registers one concrete dark theme, activates it, and contributes its vertical seal through `slots.inject('shell.overlay', ...)`, following the [slot declaration lifetime](../architecture/2026-08-05-slot-declaration-injection.md). ThemeRuntime retains the latest durable built-in preference behind the custom id and restores it when the active registration leaves; a later user selection makes the custom theme inactive and remains selected.
+
+The original WebP artwork remains in the package source and is embedded byte-for-byte as a data URI in the lazy client bundle. The plugin therefore needs no Host route, frontend public asset, or cross-package stylesheet. Translucent base and sidebar aliases expose the illustration while the existing semantic text and control aliases retain readable contrast. The seal is decorative, excluded from the accessibility tree, reduced-motion aware, and smaller on narrow viewports.
+
+ui-primitives owns one zero-Cordis `ThemeBrand` atom for the wordmark, collapsed rail, hero, Agent preset, access selector, and workspace section. ui-theme aliases decide whether built-in or custom visuals render and provide the symbol mask, display font, and seat-specific generated text. The Fang Yuan theme supplies one cicada mask plus its five text identities. `ThemeBrand` remains decorative and each owner keeps the real localized or dynamic accessible label; in particular, the Agent seat replaces only its icon and continues to display the current Standard, PTC, or user-authored preset name.
+
+The custom theme id stays process-local under the [Host-backed preference rule](../bug-fix/2026-08-06-host-backed-web-preferences.md). Light, Dark, and System remain the only durable Appearance values; installing a character theme does not add a deployment-specific id to the shared settings schema.
+
+## Verification
+
+Package tests pin theme activation, unload without a built-in preference write, slot contribution, `ThemeBrand` variant structure, decorative accessibility semantics, invariant ownership, and equality between the checked-in WebP and embedded bytes. ThemeRuntime tests pin custom-theme preservation across Host refresh and restoration of the latest durable built-in preference. The keyless browser scenario boots the real Web composition with `examples/web-fang-yuan/cordis.yml`, then snapshots module presence, dark-scheme activation, the embedded background, all five brand identities, the cicada masks, the unchanged dynamic Agent mode, functional accessible labels, palette values, seal copy, and a clean browser console.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Inject global theme CSS from the character plugin.** Competing global selectors make load order part of the palette and bypass ui-theme's semantic-token inspection and ui-layout's presentation ownership.
+
+**Enable the theme in the shipped Web default.** A named fictional-character presentation is a personal opt-in, not a product baseline; default activation would also download its artwork for every browser.
+
+**Serve the image from a Host route.** A route adds a Host dependency, HTTP lifecycle, cache behavior, and a second failure point for an asset small enough to travel with its lazy client bundle.
+
+**Copy the image into the frontend public directory.** A public-dist path makes the plugin depend on one composing application and leaves an unrelated asset behind when the plugin is absent.
+
+**Persist the custom theme id with built-in Appearance choices.** A shared Host document cannot validate deployment-specific ids whose registering plugin may be missing at the next boot. The registry remains the authority for custom ids.
+
+**Mutate the supplied XPath nodes after render.** Absolute DOM paths depend on component nesting and bypass slot, component, accessibility, and HMR lifetimes. Stable component seats consuming semantic brand aliases preserve those owners and survive unrelated layout changes.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Any later illustrated theme can reuse one frame-level paint path and six brand seats without adding global CSS or changing AppFrame. The default page retains its solid visual behavior and original product identity and carries no character-image payload; that payload loads with the enabled theme plugin.
+
+The source tree retains the WebP plus its encoded browser representation, guarded by a parity test. Custom themes do not appear as durable Appearance choices; after selecting a built-in theme, a person reloads or remounts the character plugin to activate it again.
